AWSTemplateFormatVersion: "2010-09-09"

Description: >-
  Vocareum AWS lab environment. Creates the three free-at-rest smoke-test
  resources (S3 bucket, SSM Parameter Store entry, CloudWatch Logs log group)
  that prove Vocareum runs lab.template per student, plus every IAM::RolePolicy
  grant the workshop's session role needs: Bedrock, AgentCore, ECR, DynamoDB,
  Lambda, IAM role management, S3, CloudWatch Logs, and the CodeBuild/EC2-
  describe/Secrets-Manager/Marketplace/STS statement. See the comment
  immediately below for why these grants live here instead of in a separate
  lab.policy file.

  Also carries the account sweep, a Lambda-backed custom resource that deletes
  leaked workshop resources on stack Create and again on stack Delete. See the
  "Account sweep" comment below.

  Deploy by uploading this file to /voc/private/lab.template in Configure
  Workspace. The filename must be exactly lab.template.

# Why every permission grant lives in this one file, not in a separate
# lab.policy. lab.policy, standard IAM policy JSON that Vocareum merges into
# the student session role at session start, was the first design tried. A
# live test on 2026-08-04 found that several of its statements were not in
# effect on the session role: Bedrock, AgentCore, ECR-write, and Secrets
# Manager calls came back Denied even though the file granted them, several
# with an unscoped Resource: "*", which rules out a resource-pattern typo.
#
# The same statements, delivered instead as AWS::IAM::RolePolicy resources
# in this template, applied correctly on the next live test: CloudFormation
# attaches them to the voclabs role using the deployer role's own
# credentials. Once that was confirmed, there was no reason to keep two
# descriptions of the same grants, so every statement lab.policy used to
# carry was folded in here and lab.policy was retired. docs/deploying.md's
# lab.policy section has the details.
#
# The workshop still provisions everything else itself, at runtime, with this
# same session role: its own S3 buckets, its own ECR repos, its own IAM
# roles, its own AgentCore gateways and memories, via boto3 scripts the
# student runs from inside the notebook.
# sample-stop-ai-agent-hallucinations-workshop/setup/provision_agentcore.py
# is one example; the core numbered notebooks provision the same way inline.
# This template only grants the permissions those scripts need; it does not
# pre-create the resources themselves, which would just be a second,
# redundant path to something the notebooks already make and clean up on
# their own. Neo4j runs on Aura, external to AWS (docs/neo4j-aura.md), so
# nothing about it belongs here either.
#
# The one real gap left, Anthropic model access
# (bedrock:PutUseCaseForModelAccess and the session policy listing the
# current model IDs), is an account-level Vocareum setting. No
# CloudFormation resource in this account can grant it, so it is tracked
# as an open ask in simplified-perm.md instead of being worked around here.
#
# Do not restate a permission result here: this comment went stale within
# hours the last time it carried one. Every resource below is measured as
# permitted.
#
# Design notes:
#   - The three resources are deliberately independent of each other. No resource
#     references another, so CloudFormation creates them in parallel and the stack
#     events report a separate CREATE_COMPLETE or CREATE_FAILED for each one. A
#     failure therefore still tells us which of the three services the SCP allows,
#     instead of collapsing into a single unusable data point.
#   - No instance profile is created, and the only IAM role is the account
#     sweep's execution role. See the comment on LabParameter for why the three
#     smoke-test resources avoid IAM, and the "Account sweep" comment for why the
#     sweep cannot.
#   - No compute, no NAT, no VPC endpoints, no KMS customer managed key. Every
#     resource here is free at rest, which matters against the $100 per month and
#     $200 total per student budgets.

# Account sweep, added 2026-08-06. Four resources: LabSweepRole,
# LabSweepRolePolicy, LabSweepFunction, LabSweepOnDelete.
#
# What it is for. The workshop's notebooks create AgentCore runtimes and
# memories, ECR repos, CodeBuild projects, Lambdas, buckets, tables, secrets,
# IAM roles and log groups with boto3, and tear them down in their last cells. A
# student who closes the notebook early tears down nothing. Stack Delete is the
# one session-end signal measured to always fire, finished or not, so a
# Lambda-backed custom resource wired to it is the only cleanup that cannot be
# skipped. It sweeps on Create too, because Vocareum reuses a pool of accounts
# and one can arrive still holding the previous student's leftovers.
#
# Why it sweeps one kind of resource and not eleven. The full sweep is
# src/vocareum_tools/sweep.py: eleven kinds, validated live for nine of them.
# Inlined here it is about 37,000 bytes of YAML, against a 51,200 cap on an
# inline TemplateBody and a file already near 25,000 without it. It does not fit
# at any level of prose, and cutting the two slowest kinds saves only about 2,300
# bytes, so ten does not fit either. A base85 blob does fit, at 95 percent of the
# cap, and was rejected: the one part of this file that deletes things would
# become the one part nobody can read. So LabSweepFunction carries the AgentCore
# runtime log group and nothing else, which proves the mechanism with a real
# delete and stays reviewable. The other ten stay operator-side in sweep.py.
#
# A third principal, and it is not optional. The deployer role and the student
# session role are measured separately because a result for one is not a result
# for the other. A custom resource adds a third: it calls
# AWS as its own execution role, LabSweepRole, never as the deployer and never as
# voclabs. logs:DeleteLogGroup succeeding here says nothing about either of the
# others, and the 9 grants above say nothing about this one. LabSweepRolePolicy
# is the only thing that authorizes the sweep.
#
# The first run measures four deployer actions never measured before,
# iam:CreateRole, iam:PutRolePolicy on a role just created, lambda:CreateFunction
# and iam:PassRole, plus whether CloudFormation can invoke a custom resource in
# this account at all.
#
# Why this can still break the lab, said plainly rather than papered over.
# CloudFormation has no per-resource failure tolerance on a create: no
# ContinueOnFailure, DependsOn does not confer one, Condition cannot express one,
# and a nested stack propagates its child's failure to the parent. A custom
# resource that signals SUCCESS whatever happens does absorb a failure, and
# LabSweepFunction is written that way, but that covers only the invocation. The
# three creates underneath it are ordinary resources and a denial on any of them
# fails the stack. LabSweepRole therefore DependsOn all 12 resources above it, so
# the sweep is attempted strictly last and everything a student needs has already
# reached CREATE_COMPLETE. That is the whole of the isolation on offer, and it is
# partial: Vocareum tears the account down the instant CloudFormation fails, so a
# student who hit this would get "Lab is in CREATE_FAILED state" and no lab. The
# bucket, the parameter, the log group and the 9 grants would survive it, because
# failed stacks are observed sitting in CREATE_FAILED rather than
# ROLLBACK_COMPLETE (docs/diagnosing.md), and the events would carry a verdict
# for each. That is the risk this launch spends.
#
# On Delete the failure mode is worse, which is why the handler never reports
# failure: a custom resource that fails or times out on Delete leaves the stack
# in DELETE_FAILED, after CloudFormation waits an hour for a response that is not
# coming, and that pins the account and blocks the pool rotation.
#
# Two results that would not be permission answers. An InsufficientCapabilities
# failure names capabilities and no action; the comment on LabSweepRole has what
# that means. And Lambda validates that a role can be assumed by
# lambda.amazonaws.com, so "cannot be assumed by Lambda" on a brand new role is
# eventual consistency and files as untested.

Parameters:
  ResourcePrefix:
    Type: String
    Default: vocareum-lab
    AllowedPattern: "[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,20}[a-z0-9]"
    Description: >-
      Lowercase prefix applied to the bucket name and to the tag values. Kept
      lowercase and short because S3 bucket names must be lowercase and are
      capped at 63 characters. Vocareum passes no parameter values, so the
      default has to be the working value.

  LogRetentionDays:
    Type: Number
    Default: 7
    AllowedValues:
      - 1
      - 3
      - 5
      - 7
      - 14
    Description: >-
      Retention on the lab log group. A retention period is always set so the
      group cannot accumulate cost indefinitely if a later phase writes to it.
      The list is kept short so the value cannot be raised to a year if it is
      ever overridden.

Resources:
  # S3 bucket. Chosen because it is the most visible proof in the console and
  # because GetAtt on a bucket returns values that only exist once the bucket is
  # really created. The bucket is left empty by this template, so a plain Delete
  # policy always succeeds. Retain is deliberately not used, because a retained
  # bucket outlives the lab session and leaves the student with an orphan they
  # cannot see or remove.
  LabBucket:
    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    Properties:
      # Bucket names are a single global namespace and Vocareum starts many
      # student stacks at once, so the name is derived entirely from intrinsics.
      # The account ID separates students from each other. The trailing 12 hex
      # characters are the last group of the stack GUID, which separates one lab
      # start from the next in the same account, so restarting the lab cannot
      # collide with a bucket left behind by an earlier run. Worst case length is
      # 21 + 1 + 12 + 1 + 12 = 47 characters, inside the 63 character limit.
      BucketName: !Sub
        - "${ResourcePrefix}-${AWS::AccountId}-${StackSuffix}"
        - StackSuffix: !Select [4, !Split ["-", !Select [2, !Split ["/", !Ref AWS::StackId]]]]
      # Buckets are private and encrypted with SSE-S3 by default. Public access
      # block is still declared explicitly so the intent is on the page rather
      # than assumed. BucketEncryption is deliberately not declared: the default
      # is already AES256, so declaring it would add an API call that could hit
      # an unknown deny without changing the result.
      PublicAccessBlockConfiguration:
        BlockPublicAcls: true
        BlockPublicPolicy: true
        IgnorePublicAcls: true
        RestrictPublicBuckets: true
      Tags:
        - Key: Name
          Value: !Sub "${ResourcePrefix}-bucket"
        - Key: Purpose
          Value: vocareum-smoke-test
        - Key: StackName
          Value: !Ref AWS::StackName

  # SSM Parameter Store entry. Standard tier parameters are free and the write is
  # a single ssm:PutParameter call, which is about as small an API surface as a
  # CloudFormation resource can have.
  #
  # IAM decision, recorded here on purpose. This template creates no IAM role,
  # no instance profile, and no bucket policy. IAM write actions are one of the
  # most common things an education platform caps with an SCP, and CloudFormation
  # creates resources in parallel, so a denied iam:CreateRole would roll the stack
  # back and destroy the evidence about S3, SSM, and Logs that this run exists to
  # collect. A role is also not needed yet, because nothing here assumes one:
  # there is no compute, no Lambda, and no service that has to call another
  # service. IAM gets tested in its own separate phase, after the ceiling on the
  # plain data resources is known, so that a deny on IAM stays isolated to IAM.
  LabParameter:
    Type: AWS::SSM::Parameter
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    Properties:
      Name: !Sub "/${ResourcePrefix}/${AWS::StackName}/smoke-test"
      Type: String
      Tier: Standard
      Description: Vocareum lab smoke test marker written by CloudFormation.
      # The value is built from pseudo parameters, so reading it back in the
      # console shows which account and which stack instance produced it.
      Value: !Sub "account=${AWS::AccountId} region=${AWS::Region} stack=${AWS::StackId}"
      Tags:
        Name: !Sub "${ResourcePrefix}-parameter"
        Purpose: vocareum-smoke-test
        StackName: !Ref AWS::StackName

  # CloudWatch Logs log group. An empty log group with a retention period costs
  # nothing, and logs:CreateLogGroup is the least likely of the three writes to
  # be capped. The group name is scoped by stack name, which is enough because
  # log group names are unique per account and region rather than globally.
  LabLogGroup:
    Type: AWS::Logs::LogGroup
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    Properties:
      LogGroupName: !Sub "/${ResourcePrefix}/${AWS::StackName}"
      RetentionInDays: !Ref LogRetentionDays
      Tags:
        - Key: Name
          Value: !Sub "${ResourcePrefix}-log-group"
        - Key: Purpose
          Value: vocareum-smoke-test
        - Key: StackName
          Value: !Ref AWS::StackName

  # IAM::RolePolicy grants below. All apply to the same voclabs session role,
  # via the CloudFormation deployer role rather than Vocareum's lab.policy
  # merge — see the comment at the top of this file for why. Every statement
  # here mirrors lab.policy's retired statements verbatim (same actions, same
  # resources), except that ARNs lab.policy wrote with the IAM policy
  # variable ${aws:PrincipalAccount} are written here with the CloudFormation
  # pseudo-parameter ${AWS::AccountId} instead: cfn-lint's ARN-shape check
  # doesn't recognize the IAM variable form and errors on it, and
  # CloudFormation already knows the deploying account, so Fn::Sub resolves
  # to the identical value without needing a lint suppression.
  #
  # bedrock:ListFoundationModels gets its own statement with Resource: "*"
  # rather than sharing the ARN-scoped Bedrock statement below: a live test
  # on 2026-08-04 showed it stayed Denied even after InvokeModel and
  # GetFoundationModel, scoped identically, flipped to Allowed — consistent
  # with ListFoundationModels being one of the Bedrock actions that doesn't
  # support resource-level permissions at all, so an ARN-scoped statement
  # never matches it regardless of what it lists.
  LabRoleBedrockGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateBedrockGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: Bedrock
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - bedrock:InvokeModel
              - bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream
              - bedrock:GetFoundationModel
            Resource:
              - "arn:aws:bedrock:*::foundation-model/anthropic.*"
              - "arn:aws:bedrock:*::foundation-model/amazon.*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:bedrock:*:${AWS::AccountId}:inference-profile/us.anthropic.*"
          - Sid: BedrockList
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - bedrock:ListFoundationModels
            Resource: "*"

  LabRoleAgentCoreGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateAgentCoreGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: AgentCoreList
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListAgentRuntimes
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListAgentRuntimeEndpoints
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListGateways
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListGatewayTargets
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListMemories
            Resource: "*"
          # The three top-level creates get their own statement with
          # Resource: "*" for the same reason bedrock:ListFoundationModels
          # does above. They support no resource-level permissions, so IAM
          # authorizes them against "*" and the ARN-scoped statement below
          # never matches, no matter what ARN it names. Confirmed on
          # 2026-08-05 with iam:SimulateCustomPolicy: probed with
          # Resource: "*", with the account wildcard, and with runtime/*,
          # only the first is allowed. The calls currently succeed in a lab
          # session anyway, through the platform's session-role policy.
          # This template grants them explicitly so it does not rely on that.
          #
          # The child creates, CreateAgentRuntimeEndpoint and
          # CreateGatewayTarget, do take a resource and stay scoped below.
          - Sid: AgentCoreCreate
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateAgentRuntime
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateGateway
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateMemory
            Resource: "*"
          - Sid: AgentCoreManage
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetAgentRuntime
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteAgentRuntime
              - bedrock-agentcore:UpdateAgentRuntime
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateAgentRuntimeEndpoint
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetAgentRuntimeEndpoint
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteAgentRuntimeEndpoint
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetGateway
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteGateway
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateGatewayTarget
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetGatewayTarget
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteGatewayTarget
              - bedrock-agentcore:SynchronizeGatewayTargets
              - bedrock-agentcore:InvokeAgentRuntime
              - bedrock-agentcore:InvokeGateway
              - bedrock-agentcore:CreateWorkloadIdentity
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetWorkloadIdentity
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteWorkloadIdentity
              - bedrock-agentcore:GetMemory
              - bedrock-agentcore:DeleteMemory
              - bedrock-agentcore:UpdateMemory
              # Step 10 of lab-01-verify-environment.ipynb tags a memory and
              # reads the tag back. The workshop tag is how the cleanup module
              # finds AgentCore resources to delete, so without TagResource
              # nothing it creates can be found again. UntagResource stays out
              # until something calls it.
              - bedrock-agentcore:TagResource
              - bedrock-agentcore:ListTagsForResource
            Resource: !Sub "arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:*"

  LabRoleECRGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateECRGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: ECR
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - ecr:GetAuthorizationToken
              - ecr:CreateRepository
              - ecr:DeleteRepository
              - ecr:BatchGetImage
              - ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer
              - ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability
              - ecr:PutImage
              - ecr:InitiateLayerUpload
              - ecr:UploadLayerPart
              - ecr:CompleteLayerUpload
              - ecr:DescribeRepositories
            Resource: "*"

  LabRoleOtherGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateOtherGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: Other
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - ec2:DescribeSubnets
              - ec2:DescribeSecurityGroups
              - ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces
              - ec2:DescribeVpcs
              - codebuild:CreateProject
              - codebuild:DeleteProject
              - codebuild:StartBuild
              - codebuild:BatchGetBuilds
              - codebuild:ListProjects
              - secretsmanager:GetSecretValue
              - sts:GetCallerIdentity
              - aws-marketplace:ViewSubscriptions
              - aws-marketplace:Subscribe
              - aws-marketplace:Unsubscribe
            Resource: "*"
          # Step 12 of lab-01-verify-environment.ipynb creates a secret with
          # tags, describes it, reads it, and deletes it on teardown. The
          # Other statement above grants only GetSecretValue, so the create,
          # the tag, the describe, and the delete all needed a grant. Only
          # the four actions the notebook calls are listed; PutSecretValue
          # and UpdateSecret stay out until something calls them.
          #
          # Resource is secret:* rather than a name prefix because Secrets
          # Manager appends six random characters to every secret ARN. A
          # secret:workshop*/* pattern would cover this notebook and miss
          # the workshop's own secret names, so secret:* is the honest scope.
          - Sid: SecretsManagerWrite
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - secretsmanager:CreateSecret
              - secretsmanager:DescribeSecret
              - secretsmanager:DeleteSecret
              - secretsmanager:TagResource
            Resource: !Sub "arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:secret:*"

  LabRoleDynamoDBGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateDynamoDBGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: DynamoDB
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - dynamodb:CreateTable
              - dynamodb:DeleteTable
              - dynamodb:DescribeTable
              - dynamodb:PutItem
              - dynamodb:GetItem
              - dynamodb:UpdateItem
              - dynamodb:DeleteItem
              - dynamodb:Scan
              - dynamodb:Query
              - dynamodb:BatchWriteItem
              - dynamodb:ListTables
              - dynamodb:TagResource
            Resource: !Sub "arn:aws:dynamodb:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:table/workshop-*"

  LabRoleLambdaGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateLambdaGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: Lambda
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - lambda:CreateFunction
              - lambda:DeleteFunction
              - lambda:GetFunction
              - lambda:InvokeFunction
              - lambda:UpdateFunctionCode
              - lambda:UpdateFunctionConfiguration
              - lambda:AddPermission
              - lambda:RemovePermission
              - lambda:TagResource
              - lambda:PublishLayerVersion
              - lambda:GetLayerVersion
              - lambda:DeleteLayerVersion
              - lambda:ListLayerVersions
              - lambda:ListFunctions
            Resource:
              - !Sub "arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:function:hotel-booking-*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:layer:workshop-*"

  LabRoleIAMGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateIAMGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: IAM
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - iam:CreateRole
              - iam:DeleteRole
              - iam:GetRole
              - iam:PutRolePolicy
              - iam:DeleteRolePolicy
              - iam:AttachRolePolicy
              - iam:DetachRolePolicy
              - iam:PassRole
              - iam:TagRole
              - iam:ListAttachedRolePolicies
              - iam:ListRolePolicies
              - iam:GetRolePolicy
            Resource:
              - !Sub "arn:aws:iam::${AWS::AccountId}:role/workshop-*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:iam::${AWS::AccountId}:role/AmazonBedrockAgentCoreSDK*"
          - Sid: IAMServiceLinkedRole
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - iam:CreateServiceLinkedRole
            Resource: !Sub "arn:aws:iam::${AWS::AccountId}:role/aws-service-role/*agentcore*"
            Condition:
              StringLike:
                iam:AWSServiceName: "*agentcore*"

  LabRoleS3Grant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateS3Grant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: S3
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - s3:CreateBucket
              - s3:PutObject
              - s3:GetObject
              - s3:DeleteObject
              - s3:ListBucket
              - s3:DeleteBucket
              - s3:PutBucketPolicy
              - s3:GetBucketLocation
              - s3:PutLifecycleConfiguration
              - s3:GetLifecycleConfiguration
              - s3:PutBucketVersioning
              - s3:GetBucketVersioning
              # The build-source bucket is the one thing the notebook depends on
              # that CreateBucket cannot tag in its own request, and PutObject
              # carrying a Tagging value authorizes against s3:PutObjectTagging
              # as well as s3:PutObject. Without these two, step 7 of
              # lab-01-verify-environment.ipynb is refused outright rather than
              # storing an untagged object, and the tag half of the step 14
              # sweep never sees the bucket at all.
              - s3:PutBucketTagging
              - s3:GetBucketTagging
              - s3:PutObjectTagging
            # Object actions authorize against arn:aws:s3:::bucket/key, not
            # against the bucket ARN, so the bucket-only list left
            # s3:PutObject and s3:DeleteObject with no matching resource and
            # step 7 of lab-01-verify-environment.ipynb could not upload the
            # CodeBuild source zip. Bucket actions ignore the /* entries and
            # object actions ignore the bucket entries, so one combined list
            # covers both without a second statement.
            Resource:
              - "arn:aws:s3:::workshop-*"
              - "arn:aws:s3:::bedrock-agentcore-*"
              - "arn:aws:s3:::amazonbedrockagentcoresdk*"
              - "arn:aws:s3:::workshop-*/*"
              - "arn:aws:s3:::bedrock-agentcore-*/*"
              - "arn:aws:s3:::amazonbedrockagentcoresdk*/*"

  LabRoleLogsGrant:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: voclabs
      PolicyName: LabTemplateLogsGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Sid: Logs
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - logs:CreateLogGroup
              - logs:CreateLogStream
              - logs:PutLogEvents
              - logs:DescribeLogGroups
              - logs:DescribeLogStreams
              - logs:PutDeliverySource
              - logs:PutResourcePolicy
              - logs:CreateDelivery
              - logs:PutDeliveryDestination
              - logs:DeleteLogGroup
              # The step 14 sweep reads a log group's tags before deleting it.
              # AgentCore creates these groups itself and creates them untagged,
              # so the name prefix is what actually selects them and a refused
              # read is survivable. It is granted so the tag half is a real
              # second opinion rather than a call that always answers "no".
              - logs:ListTagsForResource
            Resource:
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:/aws/lambda/hotel-booking-*:*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:/aws/bedrock-agentcore/*:*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:aws/spans:*"
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:/aws/application-signals/data:*"

  # The account sweep, all four resources below. The "Account sweep" comment at
  # the top of this file is the reasoning; read it before changing any of them.
  #
  # No name is set on the role or the function, and that is load-bearing twice.
  #
  # Capabilities. An AWS::IAM::Role needs CAPABILITY_IAM on CreateStack and a
  # custom-named one needs CAPABILITY_NAMED_IAM. Which Vocareum passes is
  # unmeasured, and the 9 grants above are no evidence: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy is
  # absent from CloudFormation's IAM capability list and has never needed one,
  # checked against the CreateStack API reference on 2026-08-06. A generated name
  # is accepted under either capability and a fixed one only under the stricter,
  # so generating it survives two of three cases instead of one. Guessing wrong
  # rejects the whole CreateStack rather than failing one resource: no lab for the
  # student and no answer either.
  #
  # And the sweeper cannot target its own executor. The generated name is
  # <stack-name>-LabSweepRole-<suffix> and Vocareum stack names begin with a c and
  # a digit run, so it can never match an entry in sweep.py's IAM_ROLE_PREFIXES,
  # where roles are selected on name prefix only and never on a tag. That holds
  # independently of the stack-owned exclusion, which is the belt to this brace.
  LabSweepRole:
    Type: AWS::IAM::Role
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    # Attempted strictly last, so a denial here cannot abort an in-flight create
    # of anything a student needs. This is the whole of the isolation
    # CloudFormation offers on a create.
    DependsOn:
      - LabBucket
      - LabParameter
      - LabLogGroup
      - LabRoleBedrockGrant
      - LabRoleAgentCoreGrant
      - LabRoleECRGrant
      - LabRoleOtherGrant
      - LabRoleDynamoDBGrant
      - LabRoleLambdaGrant
      - LabRoleIAMGrant
      - LabRoleS3Grant
      - LabRoleLogsGrant
    Properties:
      Description: >-
        Execution role for the account sweep. A third principal, distinct from
        the template deployer and from the voclabs session role, and a permission
        result for it is a result for it alone.
      AssumeRolePolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Effect: Allow
            Principal:
              Service: lambda.amazonaws.com
            Action: sts:AssumeRole

  # Its own resource rather than folded into the role's Policies block, so the
  # events report iam:CreateRole and iam:PutRolePolicy separately instead of
  # collapsing two unmeasured deployer actions into one verdict.
  LabSweepRolePolicy:
    Type: AWS::IAM::RolePolicy
    Properties:
      RoleName: !Ref LabSweepRole
      PolicyName: LabTemplateSweepGrant
      PolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          # The only destructive permission the sweep holds, and the narrowness
          # is the point: a bug in the handler's prefix matching still cannot
          # reach a log group outside this path, because IAM refuses it.
          - Sid: SweepDeleteAgentCoreRuntimeLogGroups
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - logs:DeleteLogGroup
            Resource:
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:/aws/bedrock-agentcore/runtimes/*:*"
          # Unscoped on purpose, and it is a read. DescribeLogGroups is a list
          # whose whole result set is what gets filtered, and scoping a list to
          # the path being listed is the kind of thing that returns an empty
          # result rather than an error. An empty result would read as "the
          # account is clean" and mask the delete this launch is measuring.
          - Sid: SweepListLogGroups
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - logs:DescribeLogGroups
            Resource: "*"
          # The function's own log group. Lambda creates it lazily, so without
          # this the sweep still runs and its report is lost, which for a
          # Delete-time sweep means lost for good. It cannot be scoped tighter
          # than /aws/lambda/* because the function name is generated.
          - Sid: SweepOwnLogs
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - logs:CreateLogGroup
              - logs:CreateLogStream
              - logs:PutLogEvents
            Resource:
              - !Sub "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:${AWS::AccountId}:log-group:/aws/lambda/*:*"
          # Reads which resources this stack owns, so the sweep leaves them
          # alone. The handler reports a refusal here and carries on, which is
          # safe only because this template declares nothing under the AgentCore
          # runtimes path. stack_owned's docstring says what must change first.
          - Sid: SweepReadOwnStack
            Effect: Allow
            Action:
              - cloudformation:ListStackResources
            Resource:
              - !Ref AWS::StackId

  # No AWS::Logs::LogGroup is declared for this function, deliberately: Lambda
  # creates its group lazily, so leaving it undeclared means a Logs denial cannot
  # fail the stack. The price, which lab-probe.template already records, is that
  # CloudFormation does not own the group, so /aws/lambda/<generated-name>
  # outlives the stack and is itself a candidate for a wider sweep later. Timeout
  # is sized for one synchronous delete with no wait loop to fund, and the handler
  # reserves the last 20 seconds of the clock to send its response.
  LabSweepFunction:
    Type: AWS::Lambda::Function
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    Properties:
      Runtime: python3.13
      Handler: index.handler
      Role: !GetAtt LabSweepRole.Arn
      MemorySize: 256
      Timeout: 120
      Description: >-
        Deletes leaked workshop AgentCore runtime log groups on stack Create and
        again on stack Delete. Reports SUCCESS on every path by design.
      Code:
        ZipFile: |
          """Delete leaked workshop log groups on stack Create and again on stack Delete.

          The "Account sweep" comment in lab.template carries the design reasoning. Read
          it before changing anything here.

          The one invariant: every path responds SUCCESS. A custom resource that fails or
          times out on Delete leaves the stack in DELETE_FAILED, after CloudFormation
          waits an hour for a response that is not coming, which pins the account and
          blocks the pool rotation. Refusals, crashes and a spent clock are all reported
          in the response Data and in this function's log group instead of raised.
          """

          import json
          import urllib.request

          # Mirrors sweep.py's AGENTCORE_RUNTIME_LOG_PREFIX and NAME_PREFIXES, combined the
          # same way. Change these when those change.
          LOG_PATH = "/aws/bedrock-agentcore/runtimes/"
          NAME_PREFIXES = (
              "verify",
              "wsverify",
              "workshop-",
              "bedrock-agentcore-verify",
              "hotel-booking-verify",
              "agentcoreprobe",
              "phase3probe",
              "AmazonBedrockAgentCoreSDK",
          )
          LOG_GROUP_PREFIXES = tuple(LOG_PATH + prefix for prefix in NAME_PREFIXES)

          # The documented shape is LOG_PATH + <agentRuntimeId>-<endpointName> +
          # "/runtime-logs". The id leads, and an AgentCore id is <name>-<suffix>, so the
          # workshop name leads the part after LOG_PATH and the prefixes above match. The
          # trailing /runtime-logs is easy to forget, which is why nothing here ever builds
          # one of these names: they are listed, and the listed name is used.

          REGION = "us-east-1"

          # Clock left unspent so the response is still sent after a long sweep.
          RESERVE_MILLIS = 20000

          # Fixed for the life of the stack. A different PhysicalResourceId on Update earns
          # an extra Delete for the old one, sweeping when nothing asked for it.
          PHYSICAL_ID = "lab-template-sweep"


          def remaining(context):
              """Milliseconds of usable clock left, ignoring the reserve."""
              try:
                  return context.get_remaining_time_in_millis() - RESERVE_MILLIS
              except Exception:  # a manual invoke has no real context
                  return 60000


          def respond(event, status, reason, data):
              """Tell CloudFormation the resource is done. Never raises.

              Called from a finally block, where raising would mask the exception that sent
              us there and still leave CloudFormation waiting.
              """
              url = event.get("ResponseURL")
              if not url:
                  print("no ResponseURL, so this is a manual invoke: nothing to signal")
                  return
              body = json.dumps(
                  {
                      "Status": status,
                      "Reason": reason[:1000],
                      "PhysicalResourceId": PHYSICAL_ID,
                      "StackId": event.get("StackId", ""),
                      "RequestId": event.get("RequestId", ""),
                      "LogicalResourceId": event.get("LogicalResourceId", ""),
                      "NoEcho": False,
                      "Data": data,
                  }
              ).encode()
              request = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, method="PUT")
              request.add_header("content-type", "")
              request.add_header("content-length", str(len(body)))
              try:
                  with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=15) as answer:
                      print(f"signalled {status}, CloudFormation answered {answer.status}")
              except Exception as error:  # nothing left to try if this fails
                  print(f"could not signal {status}: {type(error).__name__} {error}")


          def stack_owned(session, stack_id):
              """Physical ids this stack owns, so the sweep leaves them alone.

              Returns an empty set when it cannot be read, which is safe here and not in
              general: this template declares nothing under LOG_PATH, so nothing it owns
              can match LOG_GROUP_PREFIXES. A kind added later whose names could collide
              with a stack resource must treat an unreadable answer as a reason to delete
              nothing at all.
              """
              owned = set()
              if not stack_id:
                  return owned
              try:
                  client = session.client("cloudformation", region_name=REGION)
                  paginator = client.get_paginator("list_stack_resources")
                  for page in paginator.paginate(StackName=stack_id):
                      for item in page.get("StackResourceSummaries", []):
                          identifier = item.get("PhysicalResourceId")
                          if identifier:
                              owned.add(str(identifier))
              except Exception as error:  # reported, not fatal, see above
                  print(f"stack-owned exclusion unreadable: {type(error).__name__} {error}")
              return owned


          def collect(logs):
              """Every log group under the AgentCore runtimes path.

              Listed by the service's path because the workshop name sits in the middle of
              the group name, then filtered on the whole name by selected() below.
              """
              found = []
              paginator = logs.get_paginator("describe_log_groups")
              for page in paginator.paginate(logGroupNamePrefix=LOG_PATH):
                  for item in page.get("logGroups", []):
                      name = str(item.get("logGroupName", ""))
                      if name:
                          found.append(name)
              return found


          def selected(names, owned):
              """Split listed groups into what will be deleted and what will not, and why."""
              targets, skipped = [], []
              for name in names:
                  if not name.startswith(LOG_GROUP_PREFIXES):
                      skipped.append((name, "no workshop prefix"))
                  elif name in owned:
                      skipped.append((name, "owned by this stack"))
                  else:
                      targets.append(name)
              return targets, skipped


          def gone(logs, name):
              """True when the group is really absent, not merely deleted without error.

              describe_log_groups answers a name that is gone with an empty list and no
              error, so absence is the empty result rather than an exception.
              """
              try:
                  answer = logs.describe_log_groups(logGroupNamePrefix=name, limit=50)
              except Exception as error:  # unconfirmed is not confirmed gone
                  print(f"could not confirm {name}: {type(error).__name__} {error}")
                  return False
              return not any(
                  str(item.get("logGroupName", "")) == name
                  for item in answer.get("logGroups", [])
              )


          def sweep(event, context):
              """Delete every workshop log group the account has left behind.

              Returns a report as well as printing one: the return value becomes the
              response Data, which is all a stack output can show.
              """
              import boto3

              session = boto3.session.Session()
              where = session.region_name
              if where != REGION:
                  return {"Swept": "0", "Detail": f"wrong region {where}, expected {REGION}"}

              logs = session.client("logs", region_name=REGION)
              owned = stack_owned(session, event.get("StackId", ""))
              listed = collect(logs)
              targets, skipped = selected(listed, owned)
              print(f"listed {len(listed)} under {LOG_PATH}, selected {len(targets)}")
              for name, why in skipped:
                  print(f"  left alone: {name} ({why})")

              deleted, failed, present, ran_out = 0, [], [], False
              for name in targets:
                  if remaining(context) <= 0:
                      ran_out = True
                      print(f"out of clock with {len(targets) - deleted - len(failed)} left")
                      break
                  try:
                      logs.delete_log_group(logGroupName=name)
                  except logs.exceptions.ResourceNotFoundException:
                      print(f"  already gone: {name}")
                      deleted += 1
                      continue
                  except Exception as error:  # a refusal is a result, not a crash
                      code = type(error).__name__
                      print(f"  REFUSED {name}: {code} {error}")
                      failed.append(f"{name}:{code}")
                      continue
                  if gone(logs, name):
                      print(f"  deleted {name}")
                      deleted += 1
                  else:
                      print(f"  delete returned but {name} is still present")
                      present.append(name)

              detail = f"listed {len(listed)}, selected {len(targets)}, deleted {deleted}"
              if failed:
                  detail += f", refused {len(failed)} ({'; '.join(failed[:3])})"
              if present:
                  detail += f", still present {len(present)}"
              if ran_out:
                  detail += ", stopped early on the clock"
              return {"Swept": str(deleted), "Detail": detail}


          def handler(event, context):
              """Sweep on every lifecycle event, and signal SUCCESS whatever happens."""
              request = str(event.get("RequestType", "None"))
              print(f"RequestType {request}")
              data = {"Swept": "0", "Detail": "not attempted"}
              try:
                  data = sweep(event, context)
              except Exception as error:  # see the module docstring
                  data = {"Swept": "0", "Detail": f"{type(error).__name__}: {str(error)[:300]}"}
                  print(f"sweep failed, reporting SUCCESS anyway: {data['Detail']}")
              finally:
                  respond(event, "SUCCESS", f"{request}: {data['Detail']}", data)
              return data

  # What makes any of this run. CloudFormation invokes LabSweepFunction with
  # RequestType Create now, Update on a template change, and Delete when the
  # stack goes away, which is the signal the design rests on. DependsOn the policy
  # rather than the function, because a Lambda can be created before its role has
  # any permissions but cannot usefully be invoked that way: without this the
  # sweep would race the grant and report a denial that means nothing.
  LabSweepOnDelete:
    Type: AWS::CloudFormation::CustomResource
    DeletionPolicy: Delete
    UpdateReplacePolicy: Delete
    DependsOn:
      - LabSweepRolePolicy
    Properties:
      ServiceToken: !GetAtt LabSweepFunction.Arn

Outputs:
  # The GetAtt outputs are the evidence. A GetAtt only resolves after the service
  # has really created the resource and returned its attributes, so a stack that
  # reaches CREATE_COMPLETE with these values populated proves creation rather
  # than echoing the inputs back.
  BucketName:
    Description: Name of the S3 bucket created for this student session
    Value: !Ref LabBucket

  BucketArn:
    Description: ARN of the bucket, returned by S3 after creation
    Value: !GetAtt LabBucket.Arn

  BucketRegionalDomainName:
    Description: >-
      Regional endpoint S3 assigned to the bucket. Present only once the bucket
      exists, and it names the region the lab actually ran in.
    Value: !GetAtt LabBucket.RegionalDomainName

  LogGroupArn:
    Description: >-
      ARN of the CloudWatch Logs group, returned by CloudWatch Logs after
      creation. Contains the student account ID.
    Value: !GetAtt LabLogGroup.Arn

  ParameterName:
    Description: Parameter Store path holding the smoke test marker
    Value: !Ref LabParameter

  ConsoleLink:
    Description: Direct link to this bucket in the S3 console
    Value: !Sub "https://${AWS::Region}.console.aws.amazon.com/s3/buckets/${LabBucket}?region=${AWS::Region}"

  # The sweep's own evidence. A GetAtt on LabSweepOnDelete reads a key out of the
  # Data the function sent back, so a populated value is proof CloudFormation
  # invoked a custom resource in this account, ran it, and heard an answer. The
  # handler returns both keys on every path, including the one where the sweep
  # crashed, so neither output can fail the stack for a missing attribute.
  SweepRoleArn:
    Description: Sweep execution role. Present only if the deployer may
      iam:CreateRole.
    Value: !GetAtt LabSweepRole.Arn

  SweepFunctionArn:
    Description: Sweep function. Present only if the deployer may
      lambda:CreateFunction and iam:PassRole on the sweep role.
    Value: !GetAtt LabSweepFunction.Arn

  SweepDeletedOnCreate:
    Description: Log groups the Create-time sweep deleted. Expected 0 in a freshly
      rotated account, which still proves the invocation.
    Value: !GetAtt LabSweepOnDelete.Swept

  SweepReportOnCreate:
    Description: What the Create-time sweep listed, selected, deleted and was
      refused. A logs:DeleteLogGroup denial shows up here, reported not raised.
    Value: !GetAtt LabSweepOnDelete.Detail

  SweepLogGroup:
    Description: Where to read the Delete-time sweep report, which no stack output
      can carry because the stack is gone by then. Composed rather than returned,
      because Lambda creates this group lazily and CloudFormation does not own it.
    Value: !Sub "/aws/lambda/${LabSweepFunction}"
